Salitre Slump
Ingredients: CUMBIA + COZY, WARM & CHILL LO-FI + Synth Lead  
Creative Catalyst: Born at an illegal rooftop rave across the drowned spire penthas-skyscrapers of flooded megacity Bogotá-Sur, 2091.

A humid, hip-swaying lurch where the scraping metal of street-level cumbia meets the velvet domesticity of cassette-hiss lo-fi, suddenly split wide open by searing, monophonic analog synth leads that cut like halogen spotlights slicing through rain-swept black water.

Genre DNA
- Tempo & Pulse: 78–86 BPM. A lazy, heavily swung weba-pocket cumbia gallop, intentionally dragged behind the beat as if wading through waist-deep brackish water, propelled by waterlogged kick drums and hand-scraped metal güiras.
- Harmonic Palette: Nostalgic Minor 9th and suspended 4th chords played on salt-warped electric pianos; warm, bittersweet Andean pentatonic riffs drifted through tape wow-and-flutter; bittersweet Dorian modal resolutions.
- Production Signatures: "Silt-Ducking" (the güira rasp aggressively sidechains tape-distorted Rhodes chords); reel-to-reel microtonal pitch-wobble simulating ocean-damaged magnetic tape; screaming, liquid-resonance analog synth leads drenched in subterranean spring reverb modeled on flooded elevator shafts.
- Dynamics: Songs open in muted, crackling domestic intimacy—muffled downpours, the clinking of porcelain mugs, cassette noise—before slumping into an irresistible, heavy-hipped sway and detonating into a dizzying, thermal-overload synth solo that leaves the listener shivering in damp warmth.

Scene & Origin Lore
The sound emerged during the Great Inundation of 2091, when the lower eighty stories of Bogotá-Sur were claimed by silt and black water, driving the city’s youth upward onto linked aerial helipads and rusted rooftop gardens. Living in patchwork greenhouse shanties under perpetual monsoons, sound-pirates rigged together salvage electronics: ancient Roland mono-synths sealed in melted beeswax, battery-powered cassette portastudios, and scrap-metal güiros fashioned from discarded titanium antenna siding. Dressed in waterproof woven ruanas lined with radiant thermal moss, ravers gather around kerosene lanterns on rain-slicked tile to dance the slump—a low-gravity, half-time shuffle designed to keep from slipping off edge-guards into the dark waves below. It is music built for solace amidst disaster: comforting, warm, and defiantly festive.

Typical Instruments: Scrap-titanium güira and guacharaca; hydrophone-amplified acoustic bass muffled under damp wool felt; cassette-degraded Rhodes Mark II electric piano; Roland Juno-60 and Moog mono synth leads routed through marine distress transceivers; water-damaged SP-404 samplers loaded with pre-flood bird calls.

